# 077 — Voice V0: audio front end (16 kHz mono, speech detection, WAV source) — done

**Type:** feature · **Owner:** tools track · **Priority:** P1 · **Status:** done (2026-10-04) · **Blocked by:** — · **Roadmap:** track G · **Constitution:** Art. 11.5, MC-5

## Problem

The system records the microphone (`audio_gw`, `listen`) but does nothing with the sound. Recognition needs a clean 16 kHz mono stream cut into utterances, and tests need a way to feed known speech without a microphone ([docs/voice](../docs/voice/README.md) §4, V0).

## Plan

- `mind::voice` (libmind, feature `alloc`): `Source` trait with `Microphone` (the audio client: `record-start/read/stop`, overflow count) and `Wav` (16-bit PCM WAV, any rate, mono or stereo); `Stream` downmixes and resamples to 16 kHz mono with a polyphase low-pass filter (no aliasing above 8 kHz).
- Speech detection: 25 ms frames every 10 ms; frame energy against an adaptive noise floor plus zero-crossing rate; start after 3 voiced frames, end after 200 ms below the threshold; utterances of 0.3–8 s, longer ones are cut; `Utterance { start_ms, samples, level }`.
- `listen --vad [seconds]` shows each detected utterance (start, length, level); `listen --wav <file>` reads a file instead of the microphone; `listen` without options behaves as before.
- Host test `tests/voice_host.rs`: resampler response (a 1 kHz tone passes, 10 kHz is attenuated ≥ 40 dB), detection on synthesized speech (the `tts` host build) with silence and white noise at 20 dB SNR: every phrase found once, bounds within 50 ms, nothing in pure noise.
- QEMU: a WAV made by the host `tts` on the boot disk; `listen --vad --wav` reports the expected number of utterances.

## Acceptance criteria

- The host test and the QEMU `listen` suite pass; the `listen` suite still checks microphone capture.
- `docs/voice` and the README describe `mind::voice` and `listen --vad/--wav`.

## Resolution

- `mind::voice` (libmind, feature `alloc`). `libmind/src/voice/front.rs` has no device code and is included by the host test; `voice/mod.rs` adds the microphone and file reading.
  - `Source` (rate, channels, `read` of whole frames, `finished`); `Microphone` (audio client, 48 kHz stereo, overflow count, capture stopped on drop); `Wav` (16-bit PCM or `WAVE_FORMAT_EXTENSIBLE` PCM, 1–8 channels, 4–192 kHz, other chunks skipped, a streamed data size cut to the file; `Wav::open` reads at most 8 MiB through the file client).
  - `Resampler`/`Stream`: channels averaged, rate changed by L/M with a polyphase windowed-sinc low-pass (Kaiser β 6.75, cut at 7.5 kHz, about 2.2 kHz transition at every input rate, Q16 integer filtering; the design runs once in soft float, which `x86_64-unknown-none` provides); 16 kHz passes through.
  - `Detector`: DC blocker, 25 ms frames every 10 ms, level in tenths of a dB; the noise floor follows quieter frames at once and louder ones slowly (at most 3 dB/s while speech may go on); voiced = 9 dB above the floor, or 4 dB above with zero crossings clearly above the background's; start after 3 voiced frames, end after 200 ms; 0.3–8 s, longer cut; `Utterance { start_ms, samples, level }` (level: dBFS of the voiced frames).
- Beyond the plan: (1) the bounds widen over adjacent frames 3 dB above the floor (at most 250 ms) — without it the weak onsets «к», «h» at 20 dB SNR were found up to 51 ms late; (2) nothing under -50 dBFS is speech — the synthesizer leaves a -55 dBFS idle tone after a phrase (a fixed-point limit cycle of its resonators, `tts/src/dsp.rs`; issue [087](../issues/087-tts-idle-tone.md)), which a detector working down to digital silence took for speech.
- `listen --vad [seconds]` (microphone, 1–60 s, default 10) and `listen [--vad] --wav FILE`; without `--vad` a file is converted to 16 kHz mono, measured and played back; plain `listen [seconds]` is unchanged. Wrong arguments print the usage.
- Tests: `tests/voice_host.rs` (in CI): resampler — 1 kHz within 0.2 dB from 8, 22.05, 32, 44.1 and 48 kHz, 6 kHz within 0.5 dB, 10 kHz below -40 dB from 32, 44.1 and 48 kHz, the 5 kHz image of an 8 kHz input below -40 dB, 16 kHz exact, stereo averaged; WAV parsing and refusals; decibels; four synthesized phrases (Russian and English) in silence and in white noise at 20 dB SNR (three seeds): each found once, bounds within 50 ms (worst 38 ms over 39 seeds), samples equal to the stream's; nothing in silence, quiet or loud noise, a 100 ms click; 10 s of voice → 8 s + 2 s; level follows loudness. QEMU `listen` suite: a 7.3 s 48 kHz stereo WAV of three phrases from the host build of `tts` (in faint noise) on the boot disk — `listen --vad --wav` finds three, each within 60 ms of where it begins; `listen --wav` converts and plays it back; `listen --vad 1` finds nothing in the microphone's silence; a missing file and bad arguments are reported; the microphone capture check is unchanged.

